Z||000000||Smokin87||01-13-2001||08:15 PM||||Why wont the tires spin in my 87 GT?  Is it just because its an auto.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||01-13-2001||08:22 PM|||| Yes<P>If you had the manual transmission you could spin then all day long. Get some nitrous and they will spin.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Predator||01-13-2001||08:35 PM||||If you build it they will spin  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Now where have I heard something like that?<BR>Turbo Boost  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.usefulcomputerinfo.com/Fiero4.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000003||Black88GT||01-13-2001||10:01 PM||||It could be a bunch of things.  For one, I know that the way the Fiero is designed (mid-engined RWD) most of the weight is over the drive wheels so your not going to get as much spin as oh say a '70 Chevelle SS.  But that is a GOOD thing.  Less wheel spin = better ET's and overall driveability...<P>Another factor is tires.  If your tires are older, its easier to spin obviously.  Before I got my new BFG's I could spin tires for about 20 feet.  Now I just chirp off the line for a split second before they grip.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000004||sluppy123||01-14-2001||01:01 AM||||When I hit the N2O they spin. <P>Go find a set of wheels and tires from a Geo Metro, I think they might spin then  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=1843773&p=29628235&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||10
Z||000005||88formula||01-14-2001||01:05 AM||||It's also because the final drive for the automatics is anywhere from 2.84-3.18. The manuals have a 3.61 for the 5 speed and 3.65 for the four speed transmission. As you know the automatic fiero’s only have three speeds.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||01-14-2001||01:17 AM||||The FD in automatic Fieros tops out at 3.33||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||01-14-2001||01:21 AM||||O-yeah I forgot about that one. I'm getting tired so I better get out of hear. I've been up since five this morning.<P>Fiero56 is right.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||01-15-2001||08:21 AM||||Yea, why would you want spin? That when I get the jump on Mustangs, Camaros. They punch it and make lots of smoke and Im long gone! Besides tire arent cheap anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||01-15-2001||10:32 AM||||According to my 1986 Factory Service manual there are 2 sets of Final Drive Ratio's for the Auto's 3.65 and 3.35. One is performance and one is economy. The trans ID codes are CD for economy and PF for performance.<BR>You can see which vehicles have which FD's by looking on page 110 of the G. witzenburg's<BR>Fiero book. Looks like the 4-cyl's got the 3.35's. In 87 the auto's were dropped to 3.33 for V-6's and 2.84 for 4-cyl.<P>(So does that mean if someone who has an 87/88 4-cyl and swaps in an 86 auto out of a V-6, That they will be faster 0-60?)<P>Also remeber you lose more HP and torque thru an auto than a manual.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||MooCow||01-15-2001||03:11 PM||||Just go get a 3.4 engine and a 4:10 gear ratio 4-speed and you can spin your tires all day long<P>                               Thanks Ken||||reg||10
